From 0ee49fb50db4860a1c03d53823d7e88179fd2c81 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:01:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 01/10] pmb.build: support Rust in cross-native2

Rust packages could only be built under QEMU or with crossdirect, and
crossdirect cannot run a build script that loads target libraries at run
time: bindgen, used by every *-sys crate that generates bindings, dlopen()s
the target chroot's libclang from a native build script and fails with
"unsupported relocation type". Such packages had to fall back to
!pmb:crossdirect, i.e. a full QEMU build.

cross-native2 can do this natively. Set up cargo for it the way the Go
support already is: CARGO_BUILD_TARGET, the target linker, a target-scoped
--sysroot that finds the target's standard library in the sysroot's rust
package (the same toolchain release as the native one, as crossdirect
already requires), and bindgen's target and sysroot. Target-scoped variables
keep build scripts and proc-macros native.

Also stop installing every build dependency of a Rust package into the
native chroot for cross-native2. That is what crossdirect needs; with
cross-native2 it pulled target libraries such as libcamera-dev into the
native chroot and built them for the native architecture.

Measured on an aarch64 camera app (gtk4-rs, libadwaita-rs, gstreamer-rs,
libcamera-rs with bindgen; 213 crates): with !pmb:crossdirect it was still
compiling after 40 minutes under QEMU; with pmb:cross-native2 the whole
package builds in 2m35s.

The sysroot goes in RUSTFLAGS rather than target.<triple>.rustflags because
abuild exports RUSTFLAGS and cargo lets it replace target-scoped flags; with a
build target set, cargo applies RUSTFLAGS to target artifacts only.

Assisted-by: Claude
